WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.090
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.050
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.150
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.770
I'm Link.
And I'm Rhett.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.500
This week, at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.680
we're going to be talking
about our preparations

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.660
for some upcoming trips.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:21.910
Now that may sound like,

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:23.810
why are we going to
talk about preparations?

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:27.820
Well just wait, these are special trips.

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.160
'Cause these trips are special.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.790
Well, I mean, we should say,
you're going on a solo trip.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:33.623
Yeah.

00:00:33.623 --> 00:00:35.440
And I'm going on a trip that

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.110
I visualized a lot in my mind.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.433
And that may be a problem.

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:43.770
But you're not going solo.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.150
But not to make me look bad.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.698
We'll talk--
Yeah, right.

00:00:47.698 --> 00:00:50.940
We'll talk about that in a little bit.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:51.773
We'll talk about the fact

00:00:51.773 --> 00:00:54.067
that I've planned my entire
trip to make you look bad?

00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:55.903
Really?

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.410
That's a sign od dysfunction.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.780
But I chose to go on this
trip first at this time.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.747
And then you were like, "Well you know,

00:01:01.747 --> 00:01:03.000
"we usually travel at the same time."

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.420
But you were like, "Well
I'm going to take my wife."

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.137
And I was like, "You trying
to make me look bad?"

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.480
And by my wife, I mean, your wife,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.430
you're not taking my wife.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.792
We'll have you talked to her lately?

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.030
I wonder what she's up to.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.840
I mean, we're leaving,
tomorrow's the big prep day.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.200
And then,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.270
so 48 hours from right now,

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:28.280
we will both be on the road.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.980
Hopefully, but there's
a lot of question marks

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.490
associated with that.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.590
Enough to talk about
for an entire podcast.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.053
Another question mark is,

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:37.823
where'd you get that shirt you're wearing?

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.690
I know this was your shirt.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.140
No, that is my shirt.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.540
There is no was about it.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.390
You gave me this shirt.
No.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.240
I have given you some shirts
that were too small for me.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.710
That one fits me perfectly.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.760
Well I feel like this shirt
is a little too big for me.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.883
Yeah.
But,

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:59.390
there's an orange one and this
one, and they're the same.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.500
And you said these don't work for me.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.350
And I'm like, "Okay,
well I'll take these."

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:06.110
They're not the same. So, not a sponsor.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:10.770
That's an Elwood shirt,
which I like, and I wear.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.980
It's got a big neck.
It's got a bigger neck,

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.840
but it also, it's a little bit longer fit.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:15.770
It's a little longer.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.020
And those work and they haven't shrunk.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.670
The orange one was like a $6 one,

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.460
like a blank that I got off of Amazon.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.330
What I'll tend to do is
I'll buy blank shirts

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:27.670
that are the same kind of shirts

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.513
that we print on here at Mythical.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.940
I don't even know what brand it is,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.200
Next Level or something like that.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.000
I'll find blanks, 'cause I
like to wear plain t-shirts.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.570
But sometimes not everything's,

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:42.700
they're not always the same
size, depends on the color.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.117
And that orange one got
a little bit too small.

00:02:45.117 --> 00:02:46.670
There was another one with the orange one,

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:47.503
it was this one.

00:02:47.503 --> 00:02:48.802
That one, I wasn't, I didn't--

00:02:48.802 --> 00:02:50.093
What am I gonna do? I go
into your rack and take it.

00:02:50.093 --> 00:02:51.410
I Didn't mean to give you that shirt.

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:52.960
Can I get it back?

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:54.301
What's in it for me?

00:02:54.301 --> 00:02:55.760
There doesn't have to be--

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:57.933
Do you have another?
The orange shirt.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:02:59.783
You can keep the orange one.

00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:01.723
I don't like orange on me.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:04.930
I gave it to you cause it didn't fit me.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.320
Will you change the color
of the orange shirt?

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:08.270
I'll dye it for you.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.040
Like tie-dye, bleach it?
I'll dye it, yeah.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.500
Just stain it thoroughly

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:15.460
to where it looks like a different hue.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.293
And then I'll give you this.

00:03:16.293 --> 00:03:17.378
I mean, do you want it right now?

00:03:17.378 --> 00:03:18.550
You want me to do this shirtless?

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:20.610
No. I do not want that.

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.210
Some people might, but I do not.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:24.220
We also need to catch up.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.080
Well, first of all, we need a cat update

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.550
because, the presentation.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.060
There was quite a build up there.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:34.300
Yeah, 'cause theoretically we
could have a kitten right now.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.780
But the plan was, when
we get, Chris and I,

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:41.080
get back from our trip,
then we will get the cat.

00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:42.639
Oh. So it's happening.

00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:43.740
'Cause the kitten will be old enough.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:47.660
We decided that we're
going to get this kitten.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.273
And of course in the
presentation it was Yeti,

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:53.830
the white cat.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:57.770
And then that cat, turns
out that cat was adopted.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:58.680
That's what happens usually.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.780
And she was like, "Well, I'll
just take the black cat."

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:04.173
which she's calling Opal.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.270
But then I, and it may have
been right after we recorded,

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.680
I went home, or a few days later,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.933
and there was this whole kerfuffle.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.330
That cat was adopted out
from underneath her too.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:19.740
You gotta act fast with cats.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:22.380
Well, we were, they were,

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:23.420
Lilly and Chris were upset

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.560
because they did the paperwork,
the adoption paperwork,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.363
the application that said,
this is the cat that we want.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:32.990
We've been in contact with the cat.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:34.950
Just to kind of clarify the whole thing.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.253
And the application was approved.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:42.580
And then the next day is when
they found out that the cat,

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.860
oh, somebody else came in
and adopted another cat.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.600
But then because they were
willing to adopt two cats,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:52.990
they were given preference
and they were given your cat.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:55.120
Of course she didn't say, "Your cat."

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.830
And that just doesn't seem right to me.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.070
It didn't seem right to any of us.

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:03.020
But ultimately, after the
person at the adoption agency

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:06.207
ended up saying, "Listen, you
know what? This is my fault."

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.810
It seems like if she had to do it again,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.400
she would have given Lilly the cat,

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.080
but she was not going
to go take the cat back

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.430
from the people who adopted two kittens.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:24.430
So the long and short of it
is we did not secure this cat.

00:05:25.507 --> 00:05:27.540
The cat has been unsecured.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.750
It has been adopted by another family.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:32.030
Just get another black cat.
Along with a sibling.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:33.517
Just get another cat.
I was like--

00:05:33.517 --> 00:05:35.070
You had already settled
for the second cat.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.990
Yeah, That's what I told--
Settle for a third.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.310
Either that, or let's just not get a cat.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.800
So, I mean, so are you saying that

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:43.800
when you get back from your trip,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.420
you're going to go and
your gonna pick the cat?

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:47.270
A new cat?

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:50.110
Lilly was so mad.
Well I understand that.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.653
That there's been no other plans made.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:55.497
I think the assumption is

00:05:55.497 --> 00:05:58.150
that there's some cooling off period,

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.170
where, whenever we talk about it,

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:03.260
she's just so hot under
the collar about it

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:05.000
that we can't get to,

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.077
Well let's actually look for another cat.

00:06:08.077 --> 00:06:09.740
And I'm not pushing the issue.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.480
I was not me that wanted a cat

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:12.720
Maybe this is part of the system.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.240
Maybe they do this with
everyone, it's a test.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.560
Let's tell them we gave their cat away

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.150
and see if they're a reasonable person.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:19.983
Got to think about that.

00:06:19.983 --> 00:06:20.816
Gotta think about everything.

00:06:20.816 --> 00:06:22.059
Lilly let them have it and then--

00:06:22.059 --> 00:06:23.510
She talk to them directly?

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:26.813
Yeah. Lilly got on the phone with the--

00:06:26.813 --> 00:06:28.290
Good for her. You know what?

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:29.123
Listen. That's what these--

00:06:29.123 --> 00:06:29.956
And I was really proud of her.

00:06:29.956 --> 00:06:31.040
That's these kids should be doing.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.050
Yeah, she talked to her on
the phone, it wasn't a text.

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:34.100
I know I sound like an old man a lot.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:34.933
Well, I am, I'm 42.

00:06:34.933 --> 00:06:35.993
I mean, 43, probably,

00:06:35.993 --> 00:06:40.090
I'll be 43 about couple of
weeks after this comes out.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:40.923
Listen.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.760
You young folks, you
can't handle life's issues

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.080
either via text or via your parents.

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:50.140
Just gotta get on that phone.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.950
Do something.
Yeah, I was surprised.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.790
Take control of your own issues.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.837
I was very proud of her
for getting on the phone.

00:06:56.837 --> 00:06:59.819
Of course it didn't help.

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:02.670
And the moral of the story is, yeah,

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:03.503
you shouldn't have said that.

00:07:03.503 --> 00:07:05.730
You should have said it
worked, convinced her.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.630
Well it didn't work, I mean,

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:11.340
so now it's just, it's been like a week

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.583
of not really talking about it.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.190
Cat cooling off period.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:18.670
So we'll see what happens.

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:20.880
Okay. I'm waiting anxiously.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.293
Now another thing that
we have to update you on.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:28.090
Again, I don't know exactly
when this is coming out,

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.690
but I do know that it
will have been a few weeks

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:35.140
since this happened, but I,

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:37.774
well, no, you tweeted,
you tweeted something.

00:07:37.774 --> 00:07:40.840
Oh, okay. The boot tweet.
Yeah.

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.060
Well, okay, yeah.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:47.060
I tweeted, this is
Saturday night after I did

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:49.530
my listening party with Britain

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:52.170
and, let's see.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.710
Where is this? Where's the text?

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:55.150
Scroll, scroll, scroll.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:56.740
Well, it'd probably be under your Twitter.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.540
There's another way to
do it, but you're just--

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:01.670
Yeah, just at 9:30 at night.

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:04.137
What I tweeted out was,

00:08:04.137 --> 00:08:07.920
"Just got a text from Rhett that said,

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.827
come to living room to discuss boot."

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.910
That's it. No period, no
follow up, no nothing.

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:17.600
And I'm just staring at that.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.520
Now don't, please don't
share the followup text yet.

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:23.057
Okay.
We'll get to that.

00:08:23.057 --> 00:08:24.360
So that's what I tweeted.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.860
I tweeted that and then--

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:29.570
Well, no, well you also
tweeted back at me.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:32.820
You texted back to me, you're
like, "Can I tweet this?"

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:34.460
And I was like, "Sure, tweet it."

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:35.900
Yeah, yeah.
You tweeted it.

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:38.240
I responded to you.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.180
The mythical beasts

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:43.830
who are tuned in to our
Twitter conversations.

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:48.190
It's a certain set. You
guys were very concerned.

00:08:48.190 --> 00:08:49.370
There was a lot of conjecture

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:51.530
about what may have been meant,

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.630
whether or not I was at Links house.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:55.410
Were are we going to discuss boot?

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:56.890
So right now we're about to clear up

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.230
the discussing boot controversy.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:00.380
And let me tell you,

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:03.970
every single possible idea
that you have about it

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:06.530
is more interesting than
the actual explanation.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:08.960
And that's why I've
let it sit for a while.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:12.423
I have not heard the actual
explanation, except the--

00:09:12.423 --> 00:09:15.900
The follow up text.
Except the followup text.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:18.170
But I mean, my followup tweet,

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:21.360
when you did not tweet anything
back to my original tweet,

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.170
just got a text from @rhettmc that said,

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:25.223
come to the living room to discuss boot.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:29.912
And then people are like,
"Seems very urgent."

00:09:29.912 --> 00:09:32.197
"Just go meet up with him."

00:09:32.197 --> 00:09:34.047
"He's waiting for you."

00:09:34.047 --> 00:09:36.137
"You sure it's Rhett this time?"

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:40.790
And then I wrote back, I
tweeted, replied to myself,

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:41.860
just to give an update.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:46.860
And I was like, "We are not in
the same house. I'm scared."

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:50.903
Which just seemed to
send people into a tizzy.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.033
But I mean, the texts that
you sent back to me privately,

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:00.320
after come to living room to discuss boot,

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:04.567
was "Whoops, sorry."

00:10:04.567 --> 00:10:06.370
"That was meant for Locke."

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:07.557
And then I said,

00:10:07.557 --> 00:10:09.217
"Can't imagine how that text

00:10:09.217 --> 00:10:11.920
"would not be strange
for anyone to receive."

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.530
Right.
So it was a...

00:10:15.550 --> 00:10:17.340
You sent it to me. You
meant to send it to Locke.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:18.560
I still have no clue

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.860
what you want to discuss boot with Locke.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.100
Come to living room to discuss boot.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:26.820
Let me tell you that the Link/Locke mix up

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:27.940
has happened before.

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:31.640
I can't, this is definitely
the funniest example of it,

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:36.630
but I go to my phone to text
someone, my son in this case,

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:40.980
and I just see, it's
like, I think I'm about to

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.700
press Locke, but I press Link.

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.280
And then next thing you
know, I've sent a text.

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:46.340
Anyway.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:50.680
So I'm gonna make this short
cause it's uninteresting.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:53.923
Locke sprained his ankle on Thursday.

00:10:55.284 --> 00:10:57.203
And he's like me,

00:10:58.169 --> 00:10:59.030
he's a hypochondriac.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:02.739
And he has catastrophized
how bad the ankle sprain is,

00:11:02.739 --> 00:11:04.350
and what it's gonna
mean, and all this stuff.

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:07.020
And he's had some issues
with his ankles. High arches.

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:09.727
And so he's like talking about,

00:11:09.727 --> 00:11:12.987
"Dad, you gotta get me one of those boots.

00:11:12.987 --> 00:11:15.627
"You gotta get me one of
those immobilizing boots.

00:11:15.627 --> 00:11:17.410
"We got to go tomorrow and get it."

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:19.587
And I was like, "Locke, we just can't.

00:11:19.587 --> 00:11:21.027
"There's no place you
can just pick this up.

00:11:21.027 --> 00:11:24.700
